1
00:00:00,440 --> 00:00:02,734
You can work with anyone, anywhere.

2
00:00:02,694 --> 00:00:05,010
Until one question stops everything.

3
00:00:04,970 --> 00:00:07,423
Who goes first: the money, or the work?

4
00:00:09,440 --> 00:00:13,769
For freelancers, studios, and small teams, finding talent is only the beginning.

5
00:00:13,729 --> 00:00:18,623
Unclear deliverables, delayed payments, and upfront risk can turn a good collaboration into a bad deal.

6
00:00:19,574 --> 00:00:21,105
Meet SolPact.

7
00:00:21,065 --> 00:00:24,472
Milestone-based USDC escrow for service work on Solana.

8
00:00:24,432 --> 00:00:28,117
Agree. Deliver. Get paid.

9
00:00:29,074 --> 00:00:30,933
We're Oscar and zhaoyan.

10
00:00:30,893 --> 00:00:33,586
Oscar leads product and system architecture.

11
00:00:33,546 --> 00:00:35,958
zhaoyan builds the smart contracts.

12
00:00:35,918 --> 00:00:39,217
A two-person team, building clearer rules for collaboration.

13
00:00:40,174 --> 00:00:42,728
Here's a five-hundred-USDC project.

14
00:00:42,688 --> 00:00:46,383
Two milestones: two hundred for design, three hundred for development.

15
00:00:46,343 --> 00:00:49,598
The client locks the full budget in the project's own escrow vault.

16
00:00:50,540 --> 00:00:53,008
The builder submits a deliverable link.

17
00:00:52,968 --> 00:00:56,827
The client approves the milestone, and two hundred USDC moves to the builder.

18
00:00:56,787 --> 00:00:59,965
The remaining three hundred stays reserved for the next stage.

19
00:01:01,540 --> 00:01:03,217
And when things go wrong?

20
00:01:03,177 --> 00:01:07,480
After the agreed response window, an undisputed delivery can be claimed by the builder.

21
00:01:07,440 --> 00:01:09,910
Missed delivery deadlines allow refunds.

22
00:01:09,870 --> 00:01:13,766
Disputes go to the designated arbitrator, who can split the milestone payment.

23
00:01:15,540 --> 00:01:16,899
Why Solana?

24
00:01:16,859 --> 00:01:19,512
Every milestone means another on-chain action.

25
00:01:19,472 --> 00:01:23,882
Low transaction costs and fast confirmations make frequent, smaller settlements practical.

26
00:01:24,840 --> 00:01:28,513
USDC gives both sides a consistent unit for pricing and payment.

27
00:01:28,473 --> 00:01:33,945
Native USDC and familiar wallets, including Phantom and Solflare, fit the way people already use Solana.

28
00:01:34,907 --> 00:01:37,239
We started with an Avalanche prototype.

29
00:01:37,199 --> 00:01:39,930
Now we're rebuilding natively with Rust and Anchor.

30
00:01:39,890 --> 00:01:46,291
Separate project accounts, milestone accounts, and independent SPL-token vaults make each project's funds easier to isolate and verify.

31
00:01:47,240 --> 00:01:49,814
Our program is deployed on Solana Devnet.

32
00:01:49,774 --> 00:01:53,971
Recorded tests cover approvals, refunds, arbitration, and account permissions.

33
00:01:53,931 --> 00:01:57,004
The browser interface is still being connected to the program.

34
00:01:58,240 --> 00:02:02,415
Next: complete the wallet-to-contract experience and test with real service teams.

35
00:02:02,375 --> 00:02:03,746
SolPact.

36
00:02:03,706 --> 00:02:09,142
Clear milestones. Verifiable rules. Work together, across borders.
